  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nFor the third day in a row, we have seen multiple rounds of \nsevere weather move across the region. The main culprit in all \nthis weather has been a slow moving upper level trough centered \nover Arizona and a surface trough (convergence zone) oriented \nnorth/south across southern New Mexico. Throw in some moisture \nfrom the south and you have all the ingredients for severe \nweather. The reason we are stuck in this pattern is a large upper \nlevel ridge centered over the gulf coast to our east. On Sunday \nthe upper level trough will push a little further east and that \nwill help push the main threat for strong thunderstorms a little \nfurther east. The best chances for thunderstorms on Sunday will be\nin Hudspeth and Otero Counties, but I can't rule out that El Paso\nor Dona Ana Counties will see a stray thunderstorm. High \ntemperatures on Sunday will run a few degree below average. \n\nFor Monday the upper level trough will finally begin to lift out \nto the northeast, but not after giving one more round of \nthunderstorms to Otero and Hudspeth Counties. High temperatures on\nMonday will cool a couple more degrees from Sunday's highs. \nTuesday and Wednesday look dry as drier air sweeps into the area \nfrom the west. High temperatures for Tuesday and Wednesday will \nrun very near seasonal averages. Late on Wednesday an approaching \nupper level trough from the west, will begin to turn our flow \naloft to the southwest. While at the surface we will see a return \nof the moist southeast winds. We will see a chance for rain on \nThursday and then a good chance for rain on Friday as the upper \nlevel trough approaches the region. Precipitable water values \n(PWs) will approach one inch both Thursday and Friday which is \nwell above average for this time of year, so they may be a threat \nfor more heavy rain for the end of next week. High temperatures \nfor the end of next week will continue to run near seasonal \naverages.",
